                <pb facs="0145" n="31" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg31"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">511.3 B 1/155: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-25T11:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 25,
                                1924—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received March 25—8:35
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>143. From Grew. First day of subcommittee on traffic in arms was devoted to a
                    general discussion of a new draft convention drawn up with the expressed view of
                    amending and amplifying the convention of Saint Germain in order to secure the
                    adhesion of the United States. Control of production or manufacture not
                    included. In reply to a specific request for a statement I indicated our
                    position on the basis of the Department’s 22, March 7, 5 p.m., to Berne. The
                    desire to meet our views appears to be almost unanimous.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
